export const debate = {"id":"scrivener-folley-hiddenness-evil-moral-narrative-2025","number":"48","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Glen Scrivener vs Joe Folley: Morality, Evil, Consciousness, and Christian Narrative","label":"Hiddenness, evil, and moral narrative","date":"2026-08-28","duration":"1 hr 21 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6BzHMdTeQww","motion":"Does Christian theism better account for hiddenness, evil, consciousness, morality, and moral progress than agnostic or naturalistic alternatives?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Folley pressed cumulative evidential and methodological objections, while Scrivener offered an integrated Christian narrative whose strongest replies remained partly underdeveloped.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"It's a narrative with Jesus at the center.","context":"Scrivener presents the crucified Jesus as the controlling narrative criterion for evaluating purported Christian moral development."},"con":{"text":"it's very difficult to imagine the higher plan","context":"Folley uses a child's malaria death to challenge unspecified providential explanations and straightforward soul-making accounts."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Christian theism","speaker":"Glen Scrivener","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Agnostic philosophy","speaker":"Joe Folley","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":74,"con":84},"sections":[{"sectionId":"hiddenness-consciousness-and-divine-encounter","title":"Hiddenness, Consciousness, and Divine Encounter","timebox":"1:54–25:38","score":{"pro":77,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-hiddenness-conscious-participation","time":"11:20","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Consciousness, rational conversation, emotion, and participation in reality may constitute pervasive divine encounter rather than indicating that God is entirely absent.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply offers a substantive metaphysical reinterpretation of ordinary consciousness, reasoning, emotion, and participation in reality, presenting them as possible manifestations of divine presence rather than straightforward absence. Principal limitation: It shifts between metaphysical dependence and recognizable interpersonal relationship, so it does not answer why nonresistant people lack awareness of God, why belief varies geographically, or why sincere adherents disagree. Live burden: The proposal must demonstrate how ubiquitous conscious participation becomes sufficiently personal and identifiable to satisfy the relational premise, rather than merely showing that hiddenness and divine presence are logically compatible. Locked score: The mixed band reflects genuine relevance, recoverable possibility-level calibration, and an imaginative Christian resource, substantially constrained by unstable terminology, limited independent warrant, and failure to contact either decisive empirical component.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-hiddenness-nonresistant-geography","time":"1:54","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"An all-loving God seeking relationship would disclose divine existence to nonresistant people, yet religious belief remains conspicuously dependent on geographical location.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The conditional argument precisely connects perfect relational love with accessible divine self-disclosure, while geographical variation supplies an immediately relevant observation that makes the evidential tension concrete. Principal limitation: The excerpt does not provide representative comparative data or investigate whether cultural transmission, human freedom, institutional history, or differing conceptions of relationship could explain the distribution. Live burden: The Christian account must identify a principled, relationship-compatible reason why sincere nonresistance and pronounced geographical contingency persist, while the objection must establish that the observed pattern is sufficiently systematic and unexpected. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional coherence, direct central-burden contact, careful conditional scope, and charitable qualification, moderated principally by an empirical warrant that remains illustrative rather than comprehensively demonstrated.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-hiddenness-crucified-divine-participation","time":"23:20","role":"Major direct reply","words":"The God-forsaken Christ reveals divine participation in human abandonment and supports Christian trust amid hiddenness, although this does not solve every form of absence.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly engages experienced abandonment through the crucified Christ, combining divine solidarity, personal trust, and an unusually explicit concession that the underlying hiddenness problem remains unresolved. Principal limitation: Solidarity with abandonment does not explain its permission, and the account leaves sincere initial nonbelief, geographical variation, and incompatible religious commitments substantially untouched despite their central importance. Live burden: The Christian case must show how participatory suffering supplies explanatory as well as existential value, while specifying why a loving God permits relational obscurity that Christ's own forsakenness symbolically mirrors. Locked score: The strong band reflects coherent Christological framing, excellent calibration, substantial charity, and meaningful contact with the believer-abandonment component, tempered by incomplete responsiveness and the admitted absence of a comprehensive explanation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-hiddenness-believers-and-disagreement","time":"7:30","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Hiddenness affects committed believers and sincere rival-faith adherents, making divine relationship maintenance, apparent abandonment, and unresolved disagreement additional problems beyond initial unbelief.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move productively broadens hiddenness beyond initial conversion by arguing that perfect love should sustain and clarify relationships, thereby incorporating spiritual dryness, abandonment, and sincere religious disagreement. Principal limitation: Its evidential presentation remains comparatively compressed, and it could distinguish more carefully between ordinary relational ambiguity, temporary developmental distance, mistaken interpretation, and prolonged experiences reasonably classified as divine absence. Live burden: A Christian response must explain why loving divine relationship permits persistent uncertainty among committed seekers and incompatible faith commitments, while the objection must show why such conditions cannot serve defensible relational goods. Locked score: The very strong band follows from precise scope, strong coherence, central relevance, and a charitable possibility framing, with modest restraint because the experiential generalizations receive limited systematic support.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"evil-suffering-and-divine-inscrutability","title":"Evil, Suffering, and Divine Inscrutability","timebox":"28:34–38:55","score":{"pro":72,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evil-cross-resurrection-cost","time":"32:11","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Christ bears creation's suffering and accepts its cost as worthwhile in view of resurrection, supplying Christianity's proposed higher-good framework for evil.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies a distinctively Christian higher-good framework in which God personally bears creation's suffering and resurrection provides an intelligible horizon beyond loss, avoiding a merely detached providential calculation. Principal limitation: Treating aggregate suffering as concentrated in Christ blurs bearing, permitting, redeeming, and justifying evil, while offering no necessity or proportionality account for the particular child's involuntary suffering. Live burden: The proposal must explain why each severe instance is required or permissibly connected to resurrection goods, and why Christ's voluntary acceptance can morally authorize costs imposed upon unwilling victims. Locked score: The mixed band reflects direct central relevance and a coherent narrative response, substantially limited by asserted theological premises, compressed distinctions, slightly overstated cost-benefit language, and incomplete contact with the concrete objection.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evil-apparently-gratuitous-suffering","time":"28:34","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"A child's agonizing death from malaria makes generic soul-making and unspecified higher-plan explanations for apparently gratuitous innocent suffering difficult to credit.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The concrete example of a blameless child's malaria death gives the evidential problem moral specificity and sharply tests whether generic soul-making or unspecified providential plans possess genuine explanatory content. Principal limitation: Difficulty imagining a sufficient reason does not itself establish improbability, and the argument needs an explicit epistemic bridge explaining why human moral expectations reliably track the relevant range of possible divine purposes. Live burden: Theism must offer a morally intelligible account of this victim's suffering without dissolving its severity into aggregate goods, while the objection must justify treating apparent gratuity as significant evidence. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes outstanding salience, precision, central relevance, and appropriately probabilistic calibration, with measured restraint because the supporting case remains illustrative and epistemically incomplete.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evil-skeptical-theism-tradeoff","time":"37:01","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Appealing broadly to unknowable divine reasons may answer evil only by weakening theistic arguments that depend on intelligible knowledge of God's motives.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move identifies a powerful epistemic symmetry: sufficiently broad skepticism concerning divine reasons can block inferences from evil only by threatening favorable theistic inferences that also require intelligible divine motivations. Principal limitation: Its reach is asserted more than demonstrated, because a carefully bounded skeptical theism might distinguish knowledge of general divine goodness from ignorance concerning reasons for particular permitted evils. Live burden: The skeptical theist must articulate a nonselective boundary preserving warranted positive arguments, while Folley's challenge must show that the relevant cross-resurrection reasoning actually depends upon precisely the inaccessible motives. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent coherence, precision, charity, and central diagnostic relevance, moderated because it contacts a supporting epistemic component rather than directly assessing whether resurrection makes suffering worthwhile.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"objective-morality-obligation-and-motivation","title":"Objective Morality, Obligation, and Motivation","timebox":"42:06–54:01","score":{"pro":74,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morality-trinitarian-goodness","time":"45:03","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Eternal other-centered Trinitarian love is proposed as goodness itself, avoiding both an external moral standard and an arbitrary exercise of divine command.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The Trinitarian proposal directly addresses the grounding dilemma by identifying goodness with eternal interpersonal, other-centered love, thereby offering something richer than either arbitrary command or impersonal abstraction. Principal limitation: It asserts rather than demonstrates the identity between divine life and normativity, and it does not explain how perfect divine love generates obligation or dependable motivation for finite human agents. Live burden: The Christian case must distinguish constituting goodness from exemplifying it, establish why this nature authoritatively binds creatures, and connect recognition of that authority with noncoercive moral motivation. Locked score: The strong band reflects substantial direct contact, theological precision, central relevance, and reasonable charity, moderated by slightly overstated calibration, limited independent defense, and an unresolved bridge from divine character to human obligation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-morality-power-goodness-dilemma","time":"42:06","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"If God's power motivates through enforcement, obedience risks coercion; if goodness motivates independently, the account must explain what a personal God adds.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The dilemma cleanly separates coercive enforcement from normative goodness and asks what distinctively personal divine authority contributes if goodness already supplies motivation independently of metaphysical power. Principal limitation: The formulation underdevelops relational possibilities in which trust, gratitude, identification, and legitimate personal authority motivate without either threatening punishment or merely duplicating an impersonal standard. Live burden: Christian theism must explain how God's character generates reasons for finite agents without reducing obligation to enforcement, while the challenge must establish that personal embodiment cannot add motivational or normative significance. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent conceptual relevance, stable terms, calibrated questioning, and fair acknowledgment of alternatives, constrained by its status as a relevant nonanswer to the preceding suffering argument and by limited positive evidence.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morality-objective-condemnation","time":"49:45","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Christian theism claims an objective standard for condemning suffering, whereas Scrivener argues that naturalism cannot adequately account for suffering's genuine wrongness.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move correctly distinguishes describing suffering from judging it objectively wrong and presses an important comparative question concerning the metaphysical basis of moral condemnation. Principal limitation: It treats heterogeneous naturalistic positions too uniformly and does not address naturalist moral realism, non-theistic Platonism, constructivism, or other accounts that purport to preserve objective normative truth. Live burden: The Christian argument must establish its positive grounding mechanism and compare it against serious non-theistic realist alternatives, rather than inferring incapacity from naturalism's rejection of a divine moral source. Locked score: The mixed band reflects high relevance and a recognizable comparative thesis, substantially reduced by broad scope, missing qualifications, materially overstated force, sparse supporting evidence, and insufficiently charitable representation of the available philosophical alternatives.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-morality-emotive-motivation","time":"51:32","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Emotivist and broader anti-realist accounts can build motivation into moral judgment, while purely propositional recognition of good may leave caring unexplained.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move clearly separates moral truth from moral motivation and shows how an emotive component can make judgments action-guiding without requiring agents first to translate abstract recognition into practical concern. Principal limitation: The contrast burdens only purely propositional psychologies, since realist accounts may incorporate desire, perception, cultivated character, rational agency, or emotion while preserving claims about objective moral truth. Live burden: Anti-realism must explain whether its motivational advantage can sustain authoritative condemnation and interpersonal disagreement, while realism must specify a psychologically credible route from apprehended reasons to concern and action. Locked score: The strong band reflects conceptual clarity, calibrated scope, central relevance, and substantial contact with motivation, tempered by incomplete responsiveness to objective condemnation and limited comparative development of sophisticated realist moral psychology.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"human-dignity-narrative-and-moral-progress","title":"Human Dignity, Narrative, and Moral Progress","timebox":"61:06–79:59","score":{"pro":72,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-dignity-christian-inheritance","time":"61:06","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Modern rights discourse inherits Christian assumptions about creator-endowed equality and human family membership, while materialism allegedly cannot sustain the resulting conception of dignity.","score":63,"critique":"Strongest feature: The appeal to creator-endowed rights and human-family language identifies a plausible Christian contribution to modern dignity discourse and connects historical genealogy with a continuing metaphysical question. Principal limitation: The move overgeneralizes Christianity, secular thought, and materialism, while selected language cannot establish exclusive derivation, current philosophical dependence, or the incapacity of non-Christian accounts to defend equal worth. Live burden: The argument must separate historical influence from normative justification, acknowledge multiple causal traditions, and compare a developed Christian account of dignity with credible naturalistic or non-theistic alternatives. Locked score: The weak band reflects some relevant historical evidence and direct contact with a subsidiary bridge, outweighed by unstable scope, missing qualification, overstated conclusions, low responsiveness to prior motivation challenges, and an unsupported transition from genealogy to truth.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-progress-advance-criteria","time":"66:38","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Using Christian moral development apologetically requires prospective criteria distinguishing faithful doctrinal flowerings from corruptions rather than selecting favorable outcomes retrospectively.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies the exact methodological requirement created by an apologetic genealogy: an independent criterion must distinguish faithful development from corruption rather than merely relabeling historically favored outcomes afterward. Principal limitation: Although abolitionist and pro-slavery interpretations provide a forceful internal comparison, broader historical sampling would better establish how frequently the proposed retrospective selection problem actually governs Christian development claims. Live burden: The Christian case must provide a criterion capable of guiding disputed applications prospectively, while the objection must allow genuine gradual discovery and avoid assuming that retrospective recognition is necessarily arbitrary. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional coherence, precision, charity, evidence-bearing illustration, and direct completion of the central progress burden, with only modest limitation from leaving materialist grounding unanswered.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-progress-jesus-centered-test","time":"79:13","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Christian moral development should be tested against scripture's Jesus-centered narrative of cruciform self-giving, universal concern, and forgiveness rather than isolated proof texts.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies an explicit substantive criterion centered on the crucified Jesus and wisely treats scripture as a controlling narrative rather than a collection of detachable proof texts. Principal limitation: Resemblance to self-giving forgiveness remains interpretively elastic, leaving unclear how the standard adjudicates competing applications, balances conflicting duties, identifies future developments, or detects regression before hindsight settles reputations. Live burden: The Christian account must operationalize its narrative through transparent interpretive constraints and difficult test cases, while preserving the acknowledged moral force of its world-embracing ideal. Locked score: The strong band reflects meaningful direct contact with the demand for a criterion, clear theological content, calibration, and relevance, tempered by partial scope instability, modest evidence, and failure to resolve the decisive prospective-discrimination component.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-progress-realism-and-regression","time":"73:20","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Moral realism must reconcile timeless truths with historical discovery and provide prospective standards that identify genuine progress while detecting possible regression.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move formulates a sophisticated two-directional test for moral progress: realism must explain discovery of timeless truths while providing nonchronological standards capable of recognizing both improvement and regression. Principal limitation: Its conceptual comparison remains abbreviated, offering neither a developed realist model nor substantial historical evidence showing that gradual discovery is especially problematic for realism relative to rival metaethical accounts. Live burden: Christian realism must specify mechanisms of correction and prospective application without equating later belief with truth, while the challenge must show why ordinary epistemic development cannot adequately reconcile timeless standards with changing understanding. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent coherence, precision, charity, and central relevance, moderated by limited evidential development and failure to answer the separate claim concerning materialism's capacity to ground dignity.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":74,"strengths":["Scrivener consistently offered distinctively Christian explanatory resources rather than generic theism, especially cruciform solidarity, resurrection hope, Trinitarian love, and a Jesus-centered moral narrative.","His hiddenness reply concerning the God-forsaken Christ was carefully qualified, directly acknowledged the unresolved objection, and supplied a coherent basis for trust amid experienced abandonment.","The Trinitarian account directly engaged the external-standard and arbitrary-command dilemma by locating goodness within eternal interpersonal, other-centered divine life.","The final Jesus-centered criterion answered part of the demand for a substantive standard and improved upon retrospective appeals or isolated scriptural proof texts."],"blunders":[{"text":"The consciousness-as-encounter reply did not address nonresistance, geographical contingency, believer abandonment, or rival-faith disagreement, leaving the decisive hiddenness evidence largely unanswered.","links":[]},{"text":"The cross-resurrection response treated Christ's voluntary suffering as bearing creation's aggregate cost without explaining the necessity or proportionality of suffering imposed upon particular unwilling victims.","links":[]},{"text":"The objective-condemnation and dignity arguments generalized too broadly across naturalism, materialism, secular thought, and historical influence without engaging developed non-theistic alternatives.","links":[{"label":"Scope neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/"}]},{"text":"The Jesus-centered moral test remained too interpretively elastic to distinguish contested applications prospectively or provide reliable checks against future regression.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":84,"strengths":["Folley developed a cumulative hiddenness case encompassing nonresistant unbelief, geographical variation, believer abandonment, relationship maintenance, and sincere religious disagreement.","The malaria example made apparently gratuitous suffering concrete while preserving evidential rather than deductive force and directly testing generic soul-making or higher-plan replies.","His skeptical-theism tradeoff exposed a consequential symmetry problem for appeals to unknowable divine reasons and motive-dependent arguments favoring theism.","The power-goodness dilemma and emotive-motivation account separated grounding, authority, psychology, and action-guidance instead of treating them as a single moral question.","The advance-criteria and regression challenges precisely required prospective, nonchronological standards for distinguishing genuine development from corruption."],"blunders":[{"text":"The hiddenness case relied on plausible but comparatively unsystematic empirical generalizations, leaving the scale and explanatory significance of geographical and experiential patterns underdeveloped.","links":[]},{"text":"The argument from apparently gratuitous suffering did not fully defend the epistemic bridge from inability to identify a sufficient reason to evidential improbability.","links":[]},{"text":"The emotive-motivation reply explained practical force but left the Christian challenge concerning objective moral truth and authoritative condemnation substantially unanswered.","links":[]},{"text":"The progress arguments did not answer Scrivener's separate contention that materialism cannot metaphysically sustain equal human dignity.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that Christian theism offers a comparatively unified participatory account in which divine love grounds reality, enters suffering, constitutes moral goodness, and guides corrigible moral development.","premises":["Relational love need not maximize unmistakable disclosure at every moment, but it should provide widely accessible, cumulative opportunities for responsible recognition, correction, and renewed relationship across cultural settings.","Divine solidarity and resurrection can contribute to a response to evil only if every victim's good remains irreducible, no person is treated merely as a means, and restoration addresses the particular harms endured.","If perfect other-centered love constitutes divine life, creaturely obligations arise through the value and claims of persons made for relationship, while motivation can develop through truthful attachment rather than coercive enforcement.","A Jesus-centered moral criterion becomes prospective when communities publicly test policies for protection of the vulnerable, non-domination, truthful reconciliation, universal inclusion, and willingness to bear costs themselves.","A worldview gains comparative support when several independently difficult phenomena receive mutually reinforcing explanations, even when no individual explanation eliminates every residual objection or uncertainty."],"conclusion":"Christian theism may therefore possess a meaningful comparative advantage if its participatory, restorative, relational, and prospective claims withstand empirical testing and the victim-centered and interpretive constraints stated above."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Distributed Recognition Argument","text":"Divine disclosure might reasonably be distributed across consciousness, conscience, interpersonal love, historical testimony, communal practice, and experiences of correction rather than concentrated in irresistible private certainty. This model predicts ambiguity, but it also predicts cross-cultural points of contact and recoverable opportunities for reconsideration. Its credibility would depend on whether such opportunities are genuinely widespread and whether persistent nonresistance receives more than a verbal explanation."},{"title":"Reparative Resurrection Argument","text":"Resurrection should be framed not as compensation that retroactively makes any imposed suffering permissible, but as divine commitment to repair every violated relationship, restore agency, acknowledge wrong without euphemism, and secure goods belonging to each victim. That distinction blocks a simple aggregate calculation and preserves moral protest. It does not explain why every evil occurred, but it makes Christian hope more responsive to the particularity at the heart of the evidential objection."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that unresolved patterns of hiddenness, severe suffering, moral pluralism, and retrospective doctrinal selection cumulatively undercut Christianity's claimed comparative advantage without requiring a complete naturalistic worldview.","premises":["A perfectly loving relationship-seeking God creates a reasonable expectation of broadly available, person-recognizable opportunities for relationship, including renewed clarity for sincere believers and culturally disadvantaged seekers.","When severe apparently pointless suffering is numerous, varied, and disproportionately borne by vulnerable creatures, its cumulative distribution matters evidentially even if human observers cannot exclude every possible unknown justification.","Any restriction on human access to divine reasons should be applied symmetrically: confidence in favorable claims about providence, revelation, resurrection purposes, or moral design cannot exceed the epistemic standard used against arguments from evil.","Non-theistic moral theories can divide labor among objective reasons, emotional motivation, social practices, and institutional correction, so no single anti-realist proposal must carry every grounding and motivational burden.","Credible moral-development claims require publicly applicable criteria that could identify both a tradition's favored and disfavored outcomes without depending upon whether later communities celebrate those outcomes."],"conclusion":"Unless Christianity meets these relational, evidential, symmetric, comparative, and prospective burdens, agnosticism may reasonably withhold the claimed Christian advantage while remaining open about ultimate metaphysics and moral ontology."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Prediction Discipline Argument","text":"Comparative explanation should ask what each worldview makes antecedently expected, not merely whether it can reinterpret observations afterward. A relational God appears to predict accessible relationship, morally serious providence, and discernible developmental criteria. If hiddenness, severe suffering, and doctrinal ambiguity are accommodated only after their occurrence through flexible auxiliary explanations, explanatory fit may remain possible while predictive advantage declines. This supplies a common standard across the debate's otherwise separate topics."},{"title":"Modular Moral Account","text":"Agnosticism need not choose between objective condemnation without motivation and motivation without truth. It can remain open among realist accounts of reasons while explaining moral psychology through empathy, emotion, identity, education, and institutional reinforcement. These components need not share one metaphysical source to cooperate. The resulting account bears substantial explanatory burdens, but it prevents the comparative case from succeeding merely because one simplified naturalistic theory fails to ground every feature of morality alone."}]}},"topicCategory":"evil-suffering-hiddenness"};
